## Root cause
`padStart(input, n)` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` padded by prepending zeros to the whole string and slicing the last `n` chars: `("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n)`. For negative numbers the sign was treated as just another character, so `padStart(-1, 4)` → `"00-1"` and `padStart(-100, 4)` returned `"-100"` (unpadded). `DateTime#toISODate()` builds `"yyyy-MM-dd"` (or `"+yyyy-MM-dd"` for year > 9999) and `toTechFormat` uses `forceSimple: true`, so `yyyy` → `this.num(dt.year, 4)` → `padStart(dt.year, 4)` (src/impl/formatter.js:300-304). Hence `00-1-01-01`. `toISO`, `toISOWeekDate` (`kkkk`), `toSQLDate`, `toRFC2822` and `toFormat("yyyy"/"yyyyyy")` all go through the same helper.

Intended width convention (from repo evidence, not guessed): the sign is *not* counted in the requested width — a negative year is rendered as `-` followed by the absolute value padded to `n` digits. Evidence: existing test `test/datetime/format.test.js:57` expects year `-118040` → `"-118040"` with the `yyyy` (4) token, i.e. sign + at least 4 digits, and the task's expectations `-0001`, `-0100`, `-0010` match sign + 4 digits. (This also matches how `Intl.NumberFormat` with `minimumIntegerDigits` pads negative numbers in the non-`forceSimple` path, keeping the two paths consistent.)

## Fix (only src/impl/util.js; objToLocalTS fix left intact)
